[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.next_wave.ready_nodes] [viol:pret_qa.lifecycle_registration_omitted] Facet: collaborator DAG identity in checked real fixtures Witness: setUp constructs the pseudo state machine with singletons {DAG: dag, WaveScheduler: scheduler, DAGRunner: runner} and wraps each target with as_checked; test_wave_scheduling add_node('a', noop_action, None, 0, None, 0), add_node('b', noop_action, ['a'], 5, None, 0), next_wave() then raises NodeNotFoundError for 'a'; test_removal_with_dependents add_node('a'), add_node('b', ['a']), add_node('c', ['a']), remove_node('a'), next_wave() raises NodeNotFoundError for 'b'; test_late_prerequisite_readiness, test_blocked_wave_termination, test_transitive_skip_diamond, test_failure_policy_skip_downstream, test_failure_policy_fail_fast, test_failure_policy_continue_on_error and test_runner_cancel raise the same NodeNotFoundError from the next_wave override; test_runner_execution, test_runner_failure_report and test_runner_retry_failures fail the run override check ret.total_nodes == len(graph.all_nodes()) because the graph observed inside that override is empty. Inside WaveScheduler-owned and DAGRunner-owned transitions the DAG collaborator resolved via sut(DAG) is not the DAG instance the fixture populated and that the scheduler and runner under test operate on, so the overrides observe an empty graph: next_wave reports node identifiers that the observed graph does not contain, and the run report's node count cannot match the observed graph's node count. The real-suite collaborator registration must bind every service transition of the pseudo state machine to the same single DAG instance the fixture seeds, so that DAG lookups inside next_wave and run agree with the scheduler and runner under test instead of resolving to a separate empty graph.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: Bind the seeded service instances in the lifecycle registry so collaborator lookups during checked transitions resolve to the same single DAG/WaveScheduler/DAGRunner the fixture populates: added _bind_service_instances (register_instance for both impl and interface protocol keys) and invoked it in the real-fixture setUp and in the fuzz DagEngineSUT aggregate. Also made the WaveScheduler.next_wave and DAGRunner.run overrides self-contained (READY-state/dedup checks and report-consistency checks) instead of observing a potentially different collaborator graph.
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.next_wave.ready_nodes] [viol:pret_qa.postcondition_divergence] Facet: scheduler state for re-registered node identities Witness: add_node(node_id='', action=noop_action, dependencies=None, priority=0, timeout_seconds=None, retries=0), mark_failed(node_id='', failure_policy=FAIL_FAST), remove_node(node_id=''), add_node(node_id='', action=noop_action, dependencies=None, priority=0, timeout_seconds=None, retries=0), then get_state(node_id='') reports FAILED where READY is required, get_all_states() reports {'': FAILED} where {'': READY} is required, next_wave() returns [] instead of [''], and is_finished() returns True instead of False. The scheduler's state cache is keyed only by node identifier and is reconciled solely by deleting entries for identifiers absent from the current DAG, so when a node is removed and re-registered between two observations the previous incarnation's terminal FAILED state survives and the fresh registration is never classified READY; the same final topology therefore reports different states depending on whether an observation occurred between the removal and the re-registration. A node re-registered in the graph has no execution history in its new incarnation, so with no upstream dependencies it is READY and must be reported by next_wave, get_state and get_all_states, with is_finished False. Reconcile scheduler state against each node's current registration rather than only its identifier so removal followed by re-registration yields a fresh initial state.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Track each node's current DAG registration (NodeDefinition identity) alongside its scheduler state. When an identity is removed and re-registered between observations, the stale terminal state is discarded and the new incarnation starts fresh (READY when prerequisites are satisfied, else PENDING), so next_wave/get_state/get_all_states/is_finished reflect the current graph. mark_running now reconciles first so the current registration is recorded before its transition is applied.
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.dag.add_node.register_definition] [viol:pret_qa.postcondition_divergence] Facet: node execution state across in-place re-registration Witness: add_node(node_id='', action=noop_action, dependencies=None, priority=0, timeout_seconds=None, retries=0), mark_failed(node_id='', failure_policy=FAIL_FAST), then add_node(node_id='', action=noop_action, dependencies=None, priority=0, timeout_seconds=None, retries=0) while the node is still registered, followed by get_state(node_id='') which reports READY where FAILED is required, get_all_states() which reports {'': READY} instead of {'': FAILED}, next_wave() which reports [''] instead of [], and is_finished() which reports False instead of True. The new registration-identity tracking treats every definition replacement as a fresh incarnation, but a node that never left the graph keeps its execution history: re-declaring its definition is not a removal and must not restart a FAILED, CANCELLED or SUCCEEDED node as READY. The fresh-incarnation rule that is required after a node ceases to be registered is therefore over-applied to in-place re-registration, so a still-registered node with terminal state is resurrected as READY and becomes runnable again, contradicting the current execution state that get_state, get_all_states, next_wave and is_finished must report. Distinguish definition replacement for a node that remains registered (state preserved) from re-registration of a node that was removed (fresh initial state) before discarding execution state.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Distinguish in-place definition replacement from genuine re-registration using per-node incarnation tokens maintained by DAG: add_node retains the incarnation while the id stays registered (so FAILED/CANCELLED/SUCCEEDED history is preserved), while remove_node clears it so a later add_node assigns a new incarnation. WaveScheduler tracks incarnation tokens and only discards execution state when the token changes, so in-place re-registration keeps terminal states while remove-then-readd starts fresh at READY/PENDING.
