[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.next_wave.ready_nodes] [viol:pret_qa.overspecified_oracle] Facet: model state synchronization for raw runner execution Witness: test_runner_blocked_dependent_termination registers add_node('a', failing_action, None, 0, None, 0) and add_node('b', noop_action, ['a'], 0, None, 0) through the checked DAG, then executes dagrunner.run(None, CONTINUE_ON_ERROR, 1) directly on the raw runner instead of through the state machine's runner transition, then asserts self.checked_scheduler.next_wave() == []; the model still holds node 'a' READY and node 'b' PENDING because it never observed the run, so the interface next_wave transition computes ['a'] while the scheduler correctly returns [] after 'a' failed during execution, and the model's ret == wave assertion fails before the test's own assertion is reached. The later checked get_state('b') and is_finished() assertions in the same scenario would compare against that same stale model. A scenario that mutates the SUT through an out-of-band raw call cannot then make model-backed assertions through the checked state machine; either the run must be driven through the state machine's runner transition so the model observes every state change, or the post-run observations for this scenario must be made against the raw scheduler. Reconcile the scenario so the model and the SUT stay in sync across the run.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: Keep the out-of-band runner scenario fully raw so model and SUT never desynchronize: test_runner_blocked_dependent_termination now seeds the graph through the raw DAG, executes the raw DAGRunner.run under CONTINUE_ON_ERROR, and makes all post-run observations (next_wave, get_state, is_finished) against the raw WaveScheduler instead of the model-backed checked proxy. Audited every other scenario using raw SUT calls to confirm they either perform no SUT mutation or make no subsequent checked model-backed assertions.
